//? Title: Levenberg–Marquardt: Residuals, Damping and Stopping
//? Subtitle: A linear control before noisy exponential decay and logistic growth
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A nonlinear model is fit by reducing residuals between observations and model
predictions. Linearising those residuals gives a useful local step, but weak
curvature or a poor starting point can make an undamped update unreliable.
We derive the library's Levenberg–Marquardt rule, check an exact rank-deficient
linear control, and fit the original noisy offset-exponential and logistic curves.
A deliberately overdamped run shows that a tiny proposed step need not imply
stationarity.
)
\toc

> The method

Let r(theta)=model(theta)-y and F=.5*sum(r squared). Its gradient is J transpose r.
Linearise r(theta+s) approximately by r+J*s and add a quadratic step penalty:

$$
 \min_s\ \tfrac12\|r+Js\|^2+\tfrac\mu2\|s\|^2,
 \qquad (J^TJ+\mu I)s=-J^Tr.
$$
\alttext(Minimise the linearised residual sum of squares plus mu times half the squared step norm. Its normal equations add mu to the Gauss–Newton system diagonal.)

For positive mu the mathematical matrix is positive definite even when J is rank
deficient. This implementation solves its dense normal equations by unpivoted
elimination, rather than a QR/SVD least-squares solve. Conditioning of J transpose J
is therefore a practical limitation. Work per step is O(n*p squared+p cubed), with
O(n*p+p squared) dense storage. Parameter positivity and box constraints are not
imposed by this solver.

The retained TikZ diagram needs LaTeX/TikZ, amsmath, lmodern and dvisvgm to rebuild.
Normal documentation exports use its saved local SVG.

```{bash, file="figures/opt_lm_mechanism.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/opt_lm_mechanism.tikz > build/opt_lm_mechanism.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/opt_lm_mechanism.svg build/opt_lm_mechanism.dvi > build/opt_lm_mechanism.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.
// \image(figures/opt_lm_mechanism.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A damped local residual solve proposes a step, which is assessed on the actual nonlinear loss.)
\alttext(Residuals and their analytic Jacobian define a local linear model. Diagonal damping regularises its normal equations. Actual half-SSE decrease determines acceptance and the next damping scale.)

The native rule accepts only strict loss reductions, multiplies damping by .3 on
acceptance and by ten on rejection, then tests the *proposed step norm* against
tolerance. A tiny rejected step can terminate with a nonzero J transpose r.
This is not a full model-agreement trust-region rule. The recorder retains every
residual request, including rejected candidates; these are not accepted iterations.
Final half SSE and a separate J-transpose-r norm are checked after fitting.

> Small worked example

>> A constant curve supplies an exact damped step

Use two observations at t=zero, y=(1,3), and the two-parameter exponential model
A*exp(-k*t). The data identify A but not k: J has rows (1,0). Starting at (0,0),
r=(-1,-3), J transpose r=(-4,0), and J transpose J=diag(2,0). With damping one,
the step is (4/3,0), accepted because F falls from five to 13/9. The optimum
has A=two, F=one, with arbitrary k. This explicitly separates a well-defined
damped solve from identification of every parameter.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_lm_linear_control.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;
  d.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{0, 0});
  d.add_column("y", std::vector<double>{1, 3});
  o::CurveResidual             f(d);
  o::LevenbergMarquardtOptions opt;
  opt.initial_damping = 1;
  opt.max_iterations  = 1;
  opt.tolerance       = 0;
  const auto run    = o::trace_optimize<o::ResidualFunction>(f, o::LevenbergMarquardt(opt), {0, 0});
  const auto values = run.trace.values();
  std::vector<double> x = dm::seql(-.5, 4, 201).values(), loss;
  for (double a : x)
    loss.push_back((a - 2) * (a - 2) + 1);
  auto plot =
      dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
          .line(x, loss, "exact F(A)", {130, 130, 130})
          .points(
              values.numeric_column("x0").values(),
              values.numeric_column("value").values(),
              "residual requests",
              {40, 100, 190},
              4)
          .title("One damped step on a linear control")
          .x_label("amplitude A (rate unidentifiable)")
          .y_label("half residual SSE")
          .x_limits(-.5, 4)
          .y_limits(0, 10)
          .legend({"exact quadratic", "requested values"}, {{130, 130, 130}, {40, 100, 190}});
  plot.size(850, 490).save_svg("build/opt_lm_linear_control.svg");
  std::print(
      "{}\n\n{}\n\nFinal J-transpose-r norm: {:.8g}.\n",
      run.summary().to_md(8),
      values.to_md(8),
      o::residual_gradient_norm(f, run.coordinates));
}
```
