\image(figures/opt_lm_overdamping.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A small proposal caused by huge damping leaves a large objective gradient at the returned point.)
\alttext(Ordinary damping runs approach the exact half-SSE minimum one. The extremely overdamped run stays at loss five and returns with gradient norm four, despite its tiny rejected proposal.)

> Conclusions and references

The linear control checks the sign, half-SSE convention and damping matrix exactly.
Analytic Jacobian checks accompany the two original noisy curve families. The
large-damping counterexample separates proposed-step stopping from stationarity.
Normal equations, unconstrained parameters, local starts and the absence of a
model-ratio acceptance rule qualify this compact implementation; fitting a curve
does not supply predictive confidence intervals.

- K. Levenberg (1944), [A Method for the Solution of Certain Non-Linear Problems in Least Squares](https://doi.org/10.1090/qam/10666).
- D. W. Marquardt (1963), [An Algorithm for Least-Squares Estimation of Nonlinear Parameters](https://doi.org/10.1137/0111030).

> Reading the numerical evidence

`trace_optimize` calls the configured native optimizer through its stated abstract
interface. It records callback requests without inserting fitting evaluations.
Value calls can be rejected trials; gradient calls can be look-ahead points.
`optimization_comparison` explicitly re-evaluates selected points afterwards on
these pure objectives. Those diagnostic calls are excluded from fitting counts.
Callback indices are neither accepted iterations nor wall time. The plotted gap
uses the declared reference (minimum or stated lower bound) and a display floor of 1e-14; that floor does
not modify any optimizer update. Returned coordinates, checked objective and
appropriate stationarity/feasibility checks carry more information than a return
from a finite iteration budget. Native APIs do not expose a uniform stop-status flag.
The recorder retains exceptions and rejects nonfinite requested values/gradients;
this makes failures visible rather than letting an instrumented run continue on NaN.

